# Inheritance and the Liskov substitution principle

## Substitution is behavioral
A subtype must work wherever the supertype is promised. If an interface says a collection supports adding, an implementation that throws for every add violates that expectation unless optional mutation is explicitly part of the contract.

## Prefer composition for reuse
```java
interface Notifier { void send(String message); }
final class AuditNotifier implements Notifier {
    private final Notifier delegate;
    AuditNotifier(Notifier delegate) { this.delegate = delegate; }
    public void send(String message) {
        System.out.println("Sending notification");
        delegate.send(message);
    }
}
```
The wrapper reuses a collaborator through its interface. It does not need to inherit its storage or construction assumptions. State whether audit failure should prevent delivery: wrappers also have behavioral contracts.

## Practice
Review a Rectangle/Square inheritance design with independent setWidth and setHeight methods. Explain why preserving a square invariant can violate the parent's independent-dimension promise. Replace it with immutable shapes or a narrower interface. Write one contract test that can run against every Notifier implementation, including its documented failure behavior.

# Interfaces, abstract classes, and default methods

## Roles versus shared machinery
An interface describes a role and supports multiple implemented roles. An abstract class may own state, constructors, and shared implementation but occupies Java's single class-inheritance slot.
```java
interface Printable {
    String text();
    default void print() { System.out.println(text()); }
}
abstract class Named {
    private final String name;
    protected Named(String name) { this.name = name; }
    public final String name() { return name; }
}
```
Interface fields are constants; per-instance state belongs in objects. Default methods permit shared behavior, but they do not justify hiding unrelated business policy in an interface. Conflicting inherited default methods require an explicit resolution.

## Abstract does not mean interface
An abstract method has no implementation. An abstract class cannot be instantiated directly but can have concrete methods and initialization logic. Exposing protected fields makes subclasses depend on representation; prefer narrow protected operations when extension is necessary.

## Practice
Design a ReportSource interface and two independent implementations. Then decide whether an abstract base adds useful shared lifecycle behavior or merely duplicates a few lines. Explain how a class can implement Printable and AutoCloseable without inheriting two class implementations.

# Overriding, dynamic dispatch, super, and final

## Runtime dispatch versus compile-time choice
```java
class Animal { String sound() { return "?"; } }
class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override String sound() { return "woof"; }
}
Animal animal = new Dog();
System.out.println(animal.sound()); // woof
```
The declared type limits which methods may be called; the actual object's type determines the overriding implementation. Overloading chooses among different parameter lists at compile time. Static methods are hidden, not overridden; fields also do not participate in dynamic method dispatch.

@Override catches accidental signature mistakes. An override cannot reduce access or broaden checked exceptions beyond the inherited contract. Covariant reference return types may narrow the returned type.

## super and final
super invokes superclass behavior or construction. A final method prevents overriding and a final class prevents subclassing. Private methods are implementation details rather than override hooks.

## Practice
Add an overloaded describe(Animal) and describe(Dog), then pass an Animal variable containing a Dog. Predict the overload and separately predict sound(). Make one signature typo under @Override and explain the compiler's protection. Avoid calling overridable methods from a superclass constructor while subclass fields remain uninitialized.

# Comparable, Comparator, total ordering, and composition

## Ordering is another contract
Comparable defines one natural order for a type. Comparator supplies alternative orders without changing the type.
```java
record Person(String name, int age) {}
var order = java.util.Comparator.comparingInt(Person::age)
    .thenComparing(Person::name);
var people = new java.util.ArrayList<>(java.util.List.of(
    new Person("Lin", 30), new Person("Ada", 30)));
people.sort(order);
```
The tie-breaker makes equal ages deterministic. Returning a-b to compare integers can overflow; use Integer.compare or comparingInt. A comparator must order pairs consistently and be transitive.

## Sorted collections
TreeSet treats compare(a,b)==0 as the same set position even if equals says the objects differ. Sorting people only by age is therefore dangerous for a set intended to retain all distinct people. Use a comparator consistent with the intended uniqueness policy.

## Practice
Sort tasks by descending priority, ascending deadline, then ID. Define how missing deadlines are ordered using nullsFirst/nullsLast or a non-null representation. Construct three values to test transitivity and include extreme integer values. Explain the difference between stable sorting of a list and uniqueness in a TreeSet.

